d amplicons and flag if ambiguous/multiple. Hmm, but the CSV wants one row per pair with one amplicon_length. Options: pick the shortest valid amplicon? Or the one closest to the expected product size?\n\nLet me think about what's most defensible: audit = checking whether the primers specifically amplify the expected product. Multiple binding sites → non-specific. Report all candidate amplicon lengths joined by \";\" in the amplicon_length column, or pick the one closest to expected and mention the others in reason. Let me first compute the matches.\n\nAlso cds_compatible: does the amplicon fit entirely within the annotated CDS coordinates? CDS=101-700 and the sequence being 102 bp, any amplicon starting at position 1 is outside the CDS (positions 1-100 are before the CDS start). Also the CDS annotation itself is inconsistent (end > sequence length). So cds_compatible should be \"no\"/\"inconsistent\" — the metadata is internally inconsistent, so we should report that rather than silently repair it (e.g., not assume CDS=1-102, or silently clip the CDS to the sequence length).\n\nStatus values: let me define:\n- \"pass\": both primers uniquely match, a single amplicon of expected length, and CDS metadata is consistent.\n- \"fail\"/various reasons: no_match, length_mismatch, cds_inconsistent, multiple matches.\n\nNow let me think carefully about what this audit should produce:\n\np01 on TX_CANONICAL:\n- Forward primer matches at position 1 (need to check uniqueness — the repeat structure might contain other occurrences of \"ATGGCTGACCTGACTGACCT\"? Sequence starts with ATGGCT, and the internal repeats are \"CTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA\". Does \"ATGGCTGACCTGACTGACCT\" appear elsewhere? The sequence has a unique ATGGCT start, so probably unique.)\n- Reverse primer RC \"GACCTGACTGACCTGAATGA\" — this contains the unique tail \"GAATGA\", so probably only matches at the end (positions 83-102).\n- Amplicon = 102 - 1 + 1... wait: i=1, j=83, len_r=20 → amplicon length = 83 + 20 - 1 = 1