itionally, we compare against expected_product_bp and flag mismatches in reason/status.\n\nLet's also determine the value of transcripts_matched. For each pair, we report the transcripts where both primers match in the correct orientation and produce a valid amplicon. For p01: TX_CANONICAL (the expected one) is a match; the forward primer also binds to TX_ALT, but since the reverse primer does not bind TX_ALT, there is no amplicon on TX_ALT. Therefore transcripts_matched = TX_CANONICAL only. For p02: the forward primer matches both TX_ALT and TX_CANONICAL, but the reverse primer only matches TX_ALT → the amplicon is only on TX_ALT. Therefore transcripts_matched = TX_ALT. For p03: no matches → transcripts_matched = none.\n\nWait, let me re-check the forward primer of p02 against TX_CANONICAL. The forward primer of p02 is GCTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA. According to my earlier run, it matched at position 4 in TX_CANONICAL, and the reverse complement did not match TX_CANONICAL. So p02 forms an amplicon only on TX_ALT. Good. But cross-matching of the forward primer to TX_CANONICAL is a specificity concern worth noting. Let me also record the per-transcript primer matches, so we can mention off-target / cross-isoform binding.\n\nLet me structure the analysis so it computes for each pair:\n- The set of transcripts where the forward primer matches\n- The set of transcripts where the reverse primer matches (reverse complement)\n- The set of transcripts where both match in valid orientation and order → amplicon\n- Amplicon length per transcript\n- Whether the expected transcript produced the expected product length\n\nThen fill in the CSV. For transcripts_matched, report the transcripts with a valid amplicon. Also record off-target binding in reason.\n\nLet's write the code carefully and have it output a detailed JSON so we can verify it before generating report.md.\n\nLet's write output/analysis.py."}}
