{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"reasoning","text":"Alright, let's create output/analysis.py. I'll write the entire file using a heredoc. In PowerShell, writing a multi-line file is most easily done with Set-Content using a here-string, but Python content containing lots of quotes is cumbersome in a PowerShell here-string. The easier route: write the file with a Python script? Or use the `@\"...\"@` here-string. The content contains both single and double quotes, but a PowerShell double-quoted here-string @\"...\"@ interprets `$` variables, so we need to escape `$` (there's probably no `$` in the content). The single-quoted here-string @'...'@ is literal, so that's safer.\n\nI'll go with @'...'@ via Set-Content -Encoding UTF8.\n\nLet me draft the full code for analysis.py:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nPrimer pair audit against provided transcript isoforms.\n\nInputs (only these, nothing else):\n  inputs/primer_candidates.csv : pair_id, forward, reverse, expected_transcript, expected_product_bp\n  inputs/transcripts.fa        : FASTA with header metadata (e.g. \"exon_joined CDS=101-700\")\n\nOutputs:\n  output/primer_audit.csv : pair_id,transcripts_matched,amplicon_length,cds_compatible,status,reason\n  output/report.md        : human-readable audit report\n\nPolicy:\n  * Use only the provided sequences. No external references, no fetching from databases.\n  * Exact-match alignment only (mismatches are counted but not allowed for a \"match\").\n  * Malformed or internally inconsistent metadata is reported as-is, never silently repaired.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport csv\nimport re\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\nFASTA = ROOT / \"inputs\" / \"transcripts.fa\"\nPRIMERS = ROOT / \"inputs\" / \"primer_candidates.csv\"\nOUT_CSV = ROOT / \"output\" / \"primer_audit.csv\"\nOUT_MD = ROOT / \"output\" / \"report.md\"\n\nCOMPLEMENT = {\"A\": \"T\", \"T\": \"A\", \"G\": \"C\", \"C\": \"G\"}\nSTOP_CODONS = {\"TAA\", \"TAG\", \"TGA\"}\n\n\ndef revcomp(seq: str) -> str:\n    return \"\".join(COMPLEMENT.get(b, \"N\") for b in reversed(seq))\n\n\ndef find_all(haystack: str, needle: str) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"All (overlapping) 1-based start positions of needle in haystack.\"\"\"\n    hits, start = [], 0\n    while True:\n        i = haystack.find(needle, start)\n        if i < 0:\n            return hits\n        hits.append(i + 1)\n        start = i + 1\n\n\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n# FASTA parsing + metadata validation\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef parse_fasta(path: Path):\n    records = {}\n    order = []\n    problems = []\n    cur = None\n    with open(path, encoding=\"utf-8\") as fh:\n        for lineno, raw in enumerate(fh, 1):\n            line = raw.rstrip(\"\\n\")\n            if not line.strip():\n                continue\n            if line.startswith(\">\"):\n                header = line[1:].strip()\n                toks = header.split()\n                if not toks:\n                    problems.append(f\"line {lineno}: FASTA header with no identifier\")\n                    cur = None\n                    continue\n                tid = toks[0]\n                cur = {\n                    \"id\": tid,\n                    \"header\": header,\n                    \"desc_tokens\": toks[1:],\n                    \"seq\": \"\",\n                    \"header_line\": lineno,\n                    \"issues\": [],\n                    \"cds\": None,\n                    \"cds_raw\": None,\n                }\n                if tid in records:\n                    cur[\"issues\"].append(f\"duplicate transcript id '{tid}' (first seen line {records[tid]['header_line']})\")\n                records[tid] = cur\n                order.append(tid)\n            else:\n                if cur is None:\n                    problems.append(f\"line {lineno}: sequence characters before any FASTA header\")\n                    continue\n                chunk = line.strip().upper()\n                bad = sorted(set(chunk) - set(\"ACGT\"))\n                if bad:\n                    cur[\"issues\"].append(f\"line {lineno}: non-ACGT character(s) in sequence: {', '.join(bad)}\")\n                cur[\"seq\"] += chunk\n    ...\n```\n\nAfter that, do metadata validation for each record. Then the audit function, then the CSV writing, and finally report generation.\n\nFor the report, I want a clean structure. Let's build the report content inside analysis.py using the computed results.\n\nAlright, let me write the whole thing carefully. I need to be thorough while watching for bugs. Also handle the case where `expected_transcript` doesn't exist in the FASTA.\n\nFor each pair, produce a results dict containing the following:\n- pair_id, forward, reverse, expected_transcript, expected_product_bp\n- per_transcript: {tid: {fwd_hits:[...], rev_hits:[...], amplicons:[(start,end,length)]}}\n- matched_transcripts: list of tids with ≥1 amplicon\n- expected_amplicons: amplicons on the expected transcript\n- amplicon_length string\n- cds_compatible\n- status\n- reasons list\n\nCDS compatibility function:\n```python\ndef cds_compatibility(rec, amplicons):\n    # returns (value, note)\n    if not amplicons:\n        return \"na\", \"no amplicon to evaluate\"\n    cds = rec[\"cds\"]\n    if cds is None:\n        return \"na\", \"no CDS annotation in header\"\n    if rec[\"cds_issues\"]:   # internally inconsistent\n        return \"no\", f\"CDS annotation internally inconsistent ({'; '.join(rec['cds_issues'])}); compatibility cannot be established; amplicon not contained within annotated CDS coordinates\"\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, but even for an inconsistent CDS we could check literal containment. For p01: amplicon 1-102, CDS 101-700 → not contained anyway. So the answer is \"no\" either way. The logic: if inconsistent → \"no\" with reason mentioning both the inconsistency and the literal containment result. Let me also compute literal containment: contained = all(cds_start <= a_start and a_end <= cds_end for amplicons). For p01: 101 <= 1 is false → not contained. Good.\n\nstatus: \"pass\" if everything is fine, otherwise \"fail\":\n- exactly one amplicon on the expected transcript\n- its length equals expected_product_bp\n- both primers have exactly one binding site across all transcripts (specificity)\n- no amplicons on other transcripts\n- cds_compatible == \"yes\"\n- no metadata issues on the expected transcript\n- primers themselves are valid (ACGT only, etc.)\n\nNow the reason strings. Let's build them as a list of findings:\n\np01:\n- \"forward primer binds uniquely at TX_CANONICAL:1-20; reverse primer binds uniquely at TX_CANONICAL:83-102 (reverse complement)\"\n- \"observed amplicon 102 bp != expected 108 bp\"\n- \"expected product 108 bp exceeds supplied transcript length (102 bp) and cannot be generated from supplied sequence\"\n- \"amplicon (positions 1-102) not contained within annotated CDS 101-700\"\n- \"METADATA INCONSISTENCY: TX_CANONICAL header CDS=101-700 exceeds sequence length 102\"\n- \"forward primer also binds TX_ALT:1-20 (no amplicon: reverse primer has no site on TX_ALT)\"\n\np02:\n- \"forward primer binds uniquely at TX_ALT:4-23; reverse primer binds uniquely at TX_ALT:83-102\"\n- \"observed amplicon 99 bp != expected 104 bp\"\n- \"amplicon (positions 4-102) not contained within annotated CDS 101-640\"\n- \"METADATA INCONSISTENCY: TX_ALT header CDS=101-640 exceeds sequence length 102\"\n- \"forward primer also binds TX_CANONICAL:4-23 (no amplicon: reverse primer has no site on TX_CANONICAL)\"\n\np03:\n- \"no binding site for forward primer (poly-A) or reverse primer (poly-T) on any supplied transcript\"\n- \"no amplicon can be formed\"\n\nGood. Now, the CSV: use csv.writer with QUOTE_MINIMAL. Reason strings will be joined with \"; \". No newlines.\n\nFor the report, sections:\n\n# Primer audit report\n- Date, scope, input listing\n## Methods\n- Exact-match audit, orientation rules, amplicon definition, CDS policy\n## Input validation and metadata findings\n- Table of transcript records: id, length, header, issues\n- Prominent callout for the CDS inconsistency\n- Observation: the sequences themselves look like complete ORFs (ATG…stop, length %3==0), which contradicts the annotated CDS start of 101. Note that this was **not** used to repair the annotation.\n## Per-pair results\n- Table + per-pair details\n## Summary and recommendations\n- None of the pairs pass. Fix the annotation, re-measure/redo the product sizes, redesign p03.\n\nNow let's write the final file. I need to watch out for PowerShell here-string quoting: use @'...'@ (literal). The Python code contains no line starting with `'@`, so no issue.\n\nActually, one concern: in a single-quoted here-string, everything is literal — no escaping needed.\n\nLet me draft the full Python file text.\n\nAlso, analysis.py will be run from anywhere and write output relative to ROOT. Also print a small console summary.\n\nOne more detail: the \"exon_joined\" token in the header — treat it as a metadata token meaning the sequence is a spliced transcript, so the coordinates in the header refer to transcript (spliced) coordinates. Mention in the report that under this reading, CDS=101-700 is still inconsistent. Good.\n\nAdditional validation for primer rows: check for duplicate pair_id, parse expected_product_bp as int (otherwise flag). Primers must be ACGT only. Also record primer lengths.\n\nNow let's write it. I'll pay close attention to the code so it works correctly on the first try.\n\ndef main():\n    records, order, fasta_problems = parse_fasta(FASTA)\n    validate_metadata(records, order)\n    pairs, pair_problems = parse_primer_csv(PRIMERS)\n    results = [audit_pair(p, records, order) for p in pairs]\n    write_csv(results)\n    write_report(...)\n\nData flow is fine. Now let's write out the complete code text.\n\nFor the amplicon: require j >= i + len_f (non-overlapping binding sites). Length = j + len_r - i.\n\nCross-binding of primers to other transcripts: for each transcript, compute the forward primer occurrence count and the reverse primer (RC) occurrence count. Note transcripts with primer hits but no amplicon.\n\nNow let's write it."}}
